  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nMidday satellite imagery depicted a perfectly clear sky over \nSW KS, with surface observations showing downslope SWly winds\nprevailing at most locations. This combination along with\nseveral degrees of warming at 850 mb will allow temperatures to\nsoar through the 70s this afternoon, with lower 80s across the\nsoutheast counties. \n\nA weak frontal boundary will sag southward across SW KS tonight,\ncreating a light northeast wind shift, and allowing stratus and\nareas of fog to redevelop after midnight across at least the\nnorthern zones. Areas of fog were included in the grids,\nfavoring areas north and west of Dodge City. \n\nA large temperature range is expected Thursday, along with\ntemperature forecast bust potential at any one location. Stratus\nwill erode Thursday afternoon, but there is uncertainty\nregarding how fast this will occur. Forecast temperature range\nThursday afternoon is from near 60 north to near 80 southeast. \nSW KS is expected to remain dry through Thursday night. \n\nA subtropical shortwave arriving from the southwest, infused\nwith remnant moisture/vorticity from Pacific tropical cyclone\nVance, has been consistently forecast by models to arrive Friday\nwith widespread rainfall. Rainfall amounts of 0.25-0.50 inch\nare expected to be common with this event, with NBM pops in the\ndefinite category (>80%) across the southeast half of SW KS\nFriday and Friday night. With tropical, moist lapse rates \ninstability will be poor, as such NBM has correctly taken most \nof the thunder out of the grids. NBM temperatures are likely too\nwarm Friday, with clouds and rain holding most locations in the\n50s. \n\nDry weather returns for Halloween, with temperatures falling\nthrough the 60s and 50s Saturday evening. A light southeast wind\nis expected. \n\nModels show two strong shortwaves from the polar and subtropical\njet streams combining into one strong closed midlevel cyclone\nover the Great Basin by late Sunday. The daylight hours of the\nfirst day of November are expected to remain dry, with elevated\nsouth winds and mild temperatures in the 60s. \n\nNBM pops increase into the likely category Sunday night, as\nforcing for ascent arrives ahead of the approaching trough. A\nstrong low level jet will supply a healthy moisture supply and\nbulk shear, so can't rule out stronger storms with heavy\nrainfall potential Sunday night. Early next week, the entire\nstorm system will arrive on the plains Monday through Tuesday. \nWhile this broad synoptic signal remains consistent, the details\nof where/when the heaviest rains and strongest winds will occur\nare still unknown. Some models split the trough upon arrival \nwith a weak storm system, others keep it organized into a much\nstronger coherent trough with potentially higher impacts - it is\njust too early to know.",
